    "updated_at": "2026-03-30T14:09:03Z",
    "body": "Thanks for filing the issue! The spec is a living document, so having semantic versions goes against that principle. It's possible to follow the commit log if you're interested in fine detail, otherwise likely the most pragmatic choice would be to follow release notes of the major browsers which implement the spec, so for example you can see [What's new in Firefox](https://www.firefox.com/en-US/releases/), follow the [Safari Technology Preview Release Notes](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/safari-technology-preview-release-notes) or read up on [Chrome's Release Notes](https://developer.chrome.com/release-notes). [Ladybird also has a News section](https://ladybird.org/news/) which shows what they're implementing. There's also [BCD Watch](https://bcd-watch.igalia.com/) which can be used to follow changes across many implementations.\n\nI think sharing regular updates is perhaps beyond the scope of the spec, and perhaps more something that could be integrated into developer facing sites like [MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/). Maybe it's worth filing issues there to see if there is interest in such a feature.\n\nI'll close this for now as I don't think we've got plans for any changes in the HTML spec itself. But if my colleagues from the other browsers disagree, please re-open. We can of course continue discussing it here.",
